  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA warm front has halted its northward progression and will\ntransition into a slow moving cold front this afternoon. Ahead\nof this boundary, breaks in the cloud cover have allowed surface\nbased instability to build to 1500 to 2000 J/kg, capitalizing on\na moisture rich boundary layer despite weak mid-level lapse\nrates. Deep layer shear of 25 to 30KTs will support a mix of \nmulticell and transient supercell structures. The primary severe\nhazard will be damaging wind gusts. Given antecedent heavy \nrainfall, soils are at least partially compromised across the \nregion. Consequently, tree uprooting is likely to occur at sub \nsevere wind speeds as low as 40 mph, with more substantial \ndamage possible in any 50 to 60 mph gusts. With low LCLs in \nplace, a brief tornado cannot be entirely ruled out, though weak\nand largely unidirectional low level wind profiles suggest this\nthreat is minimal outside of storm scale interaction. High wet\nbulb zeros near 11kft should limit hail risk outside of the\nstrongest updrafts or left splits.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nRegarding the flash flood threat, while individual storm motions\nremain progressive at 20 to 30KTs within westerly flow, \ntraining of convection along the slowly sagging front could \nallow for significant convective dwell time over any given spot.\nThe most critical area for flash flooding is along a line from \nSpencer to Philippi, where additional training convection this \nafternoon and evening will exacerbate conditions over soils that\nare already compromised from morning rainfall. Elsewhere north \nof US-50, training storms may yield minor to pockets of moderate\nflooding issues. Father south across the Metro Valley, \nscattered to numerous storms early this afternoon near the I-64\ncorridor will continue to precondition soils for rapid runoff \nwith later activity, although the later activity should become \nmore progressive by the time it crosses I-64 as a mid-level \nshort wave approaches this evening. Existing flash flood watch \nin effect through 2 AM looks to cover this threat adequately, \nwith northern sections likely being able to be dropped early.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nTonight into Thursday, the aforementioned mid level wave will\nsweep the front south of the area. However, the arrival of\nsubstantially drier air will lag behind the frontal passage.\nAmidst weakening surface winds and partially clearing skies from\nthe north, conditions will become highly favorable for the\ndevelopment of dense fog and very low stratus late tonight into\nThursday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...\n\nBy Thursday afternoon, drier air will firmly settle across the\nregion, bringing a return to more seasonable temperatures and\ngenerally quiet weather. A dry pattern is anticipated to persist\nthrough the upcoming weekend. A weak backdoor cold front will\npass through on Saturday, serving mainly to reinforce the dry\nairmass. This dry air could yield some chilly temperatures,\nupper 30s to lower 40s in the mountain valleys for late May. \nLooking ahead to early next week, a weak disturbance dropping \nout of Canada may bring a return of light rain chances on \nMonday, but the overall pattern appears largely dry.",
